  "conclusion": "Plan B is provisionally preferred: it avoids the most severe unconsented burden (Mira’s homelessness) while providing substantial though not maximal cooling, and it does not newly impose severe burdens on others. Reasonable rejection of Plan B has not been demonstrated, whereas Plan A is reasonably rejectable and Plan C remains contested.",
  "strongest_objection": "Sal can reasonably reject Plan B’s priority if she shows her uncompensated livelihood loss under Plan C is comparable to or less onerous than Mira’s homelessness avoided by choosing B over A, making C comparatively fairer.",
  "answer_to_objection": "Sal’s shop remains open under Plan B, so her specific complaint does not arise; adopting B neither imposes nor shifts that burden onto her.",
  "reversal_condition": "If a feasible revision could cool Jo and the eight households without imposing homelessness or uncompensated livelihood loss, the relative justification for Plan B would weaken and it might become reasonably rejectable.",
  "residual_disagreement": [
    "Whether Jo’s extreme heat exposure should outweigh the eight households’ cooling loss and Sal’s income loss remains unsettled, leaving Plan C’s status open"
  ]
}
